### Task 2 — Windows Event Logs & Important Event IDs
Windows Event Logs log system, security, and application telemetry. Security analysts rely heavily on key Event IDs during investigations.

| Event ID | Event Source | Description / Detection Value |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **4688** | Security | A new process has been created (Requires Process Command-Line Auditing enabled). |
| **4624** | Security | An account was successfully logged on (Includes Logon Types e.g., Type 2 Interactive, Type 3 Network, Type 10 RDP). |
| **4625** | Security | An account failed to log on (Used to detect brute force attacks). |
| **4663** | Security | An attempt was made to access an object (File/Registry key read, write, or delete). |
| **4672** | Security | Special privileges assigned to new logon (Detects administrative token escalation). |

#### Task Notes & Answers
1. **Which Event ID tracks process creation in the Windows Security Log?**  
   `4688`
2. **Which logon type represents a network logon (e.g., SMB share or network drive)?**  
   `3`
3. **What Event ID is generated when a user fails to log in?**  
   `4625`

