### Task 3 — Sysmon Integration for Advanced Telemetry
Standard Windows Security logs lack specific low-level details (like process command-line history or hash signatures) by default. **Sysmon** fills this gap.

```text
[ Process Creation ] -> Sysmon Event ID 1 (Process GUID, Image Path, Parent Process, Hashes)
[ Network Connection ] -> Sysmon Event ID 3 (Source IP, Destination IP, Destination Port)
[ Process Tampering ] -> Sysmon Event ID 25 (Process Herpaderping / Injection)

Task 4 — Translating IOCs with Sigma Rules
Sigma is an open-source, vendor-neutral signature format used to write log detection rules.
# Example Sigma Rule Structure
title: Suspicious PowerShell Child Process
status: experimental
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: windows
detection:
    selection:
        ParentImage|endswith: 
            - '\winword.exe'
            - '\excel.exe'
        Image|endswith: '\powershell.exe'
    condition: selection
falsepositives:
    - Administrative scripts
level: high

Task 5 — Practical Investigation Scenario
Analyzing a provided log dataset (.evtx logs or SIEM index) to track down an intruder on a compromised host.
Investigation Timeline & Findings
Initial Access / Execution:Attacker initiated execution via a suspicious document spawning cmd.exe $\rightarrow$ powershell.exe.
Reconnaissance:Executed standard discovery commands: whoami, net user, nltest /dclist.
Privilege Escalation / Persistence:Created a scheduled task or leveraged a SUID/token manipulation binary.
